    "query": "How do I add an item to the end of a Python list?",
    "query_dimensions": 384,
    "top3": [
      {
        "chunk_id": "f9ab9a02bf3383243e332ac560b27dbc",
        "text": "5.1. More on Lists\n\nThe list data type has some more methods. Here are all of the methods of list objects:\n\nlist.append(value, /)\n\nAdd an item to the end of the list. Similar to a[len(a):] = [x].\n\nlist.extend(iterable, /)\n\nExtend the list by appending all the items from the iterable. Similar to a[len(a):] = iterable.\n\nlist.insert(index, value, /)",
        "metadata": {
          "chapter": 5,
          "version": "3.14.10",
          "char_end": 483,
          "language": "en",
          "char_start": 135,
          "block_kinds": [
            "heading",
            "paragraph",
            "term"
          ],
          "section_ids": [
            "more-on-lists"
          ],
          "unit_char_start_in_chapter": 0,
          "document_id": "python-3.14-ch05",
          "source": "https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/datastructures.html",
          "page": "python-3.14-ch05-u001"
        },
        "score": 0.8931049640837306
      },
      {
        "chunk_id": "6713445f8d29820661a5d0f210010c7a",
        "text": "Insert an item at a given position. The first argument is the index of the element before which to insert, so a.insert(0, x) inserts at the front of the list, and a.insert(len(a), x) is equivalent to a.append(x).\n\nlist.remove(value, /)\n\nRemove the first item from the list whose value is equal to value. It raises a ValueError if there is no such item.\n\nlist.pop(index=-1, /)",
        "metadata": {
